-- Add practice depth without changing existing questions, attempts, or prerequisites.
INSERT INTO questions (prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, concept_id)
SELECT seed.prompt, seed.options::jsonb, seed.answer, seed.explanation, c.id
FROM (VALUES
    ('A process is waiting for a disk read to finish. Which state best describes it?',
     '{"A":"Running","B":"Blocked (waiting)","C":"Ready","D":"Terminated"}', 'B',
     'A blocked process cannot continue until an event such as I/O completion occurs. A ready process only needs CPU time.', 'Processes'),
    ('What normally happens when a blocked process completes its I/O, but another process still occupies the CPU?',
     '{"A":"It enters the ready state","B":"It must terminate","C":"It immediately replaces the running process in every scheduler","D":"It returns to the new state"}', 'A',
     'I/O completion makes the process runnable again. It normally enters the ready queue; the scheduling policy determines when it runs.', 'Processes'),
    ('During a context switch between processes, what must the operating system preserve so the outgoing process can resume?',
     '{"A":"Only its executable filename","B":"Only the current wall-clock time","C":"Its CPU execution state, including registers and program counter","D":"The source code of its compiler"}', 'C',
     'Saving registers and the program counter allows a process to resume its execution after another process uses the CPU.', 'Processes'),
    ('Two independent processes use the same numerical virtual address. What does this imply?',
     '{"A":"They must access the same physical byte","B":"Their entire memory contents are identical","C":"They cannot run concurrently","D":"The addresses can map to different physical memory in their separate address spaces"}', 'D',
     'Virtual addresses are interpreted within an address space. Equal numerical addresses in different processes need not refer to the same physical memory.', 'Processes'),
    ('After a successful UNIX fork(), what value does the call return in the child process?',
     '{"A":"The parent process ID","B":"Zero","C":"Always minus one","D":"The number of ready processes"}', 'B',
     'A successful fork returns zero in the child and the child process ID in the parent. A failure returns minus one to the caller.', 'Processes'),
    ('What does a successful UNIX exec-family call do to the calling process?',
     '{"A":"Replaces its program image while retaining its process ID","B":"Always creates a second process with a new process ID","C":"Only changes its scheduling priority","D":"Returns to the next instruction in the old program"}', 'A',
     'Exec replaces the current process program image. It does not itself create a new process, and a successful call does not return to the old program.', 'Processes'),
    ('Two threads increment the same shared counter without synchronization. Why can an update be lost?',
     '{"A":"Threads always have separate copies of every variable","B":"The operating system forbids shared writes","C":"Their read-modify-write steps can interleave using the same old value","D":"A counter can only be changed by a process, never by a thread"}', 'C',
     'An increment need not be atomic. Both threads can read the same old value and then store the same incremented value, losing one update.', 'Threads'),
    ('Which mechanism is appropriate for allowing only one cooperating thread at a time into a critical section?',
     '{"A":"A separate program counter alone","B":"A larger CPU cache","C":"A shorter thread name","D":"A mutex acquired before entry and released after exit"}', 'D',
     'When all accesses follow the same locking protocol, a mutex provides mutual exclusion for the protected critical section.', 'Threads'),
    ('A main thread must wait until a worker thread finishes before using its result. Which operation expresses this requirement?',
     '{"A":"Join the worker thread","B":"Sleep for an arbitrary fixed duration","C":"Change the worker name","D":"Read the result repeatedly without synchronization"}', 'A',
     'Joining waits for the target thread to terminate. An arbitrary sleep does not guarantee that the worker has finished.', 'Threads'),
    ('Two runnable threads can execute simultaneously on two CPU cores. What is this an example of?',
     '{"A":"Only sequential execution","B":"Parallel execution","C":"A mandatory deadlock","D":"A single shared register set"}', 'B',
     'Parallelism means simultaneous execution. Concurrency can also occur through interleaving on a single core.', 'Threads'),
    ('In a many-to-one threading model, several user threads map to one kernel thread. What limits CPU parallelism for those user threads?',
     '{"A":"Each user thread has a separate stack","B":"They can never share data","C":"The kernel has only one schedulable kernel thread for them","D":"They must all execute different programs"}', 'C',
     'With only one underlying kernel thread, these user threads cannot execute simultaneously on multiple cores, even if multiple cores are available.', 'Threads'),
    ('Why does each thread normally need its own stack?',
     '{"A":"To prevent any sharing of heap objects","B":"To store a separate copy of the operating system","C":"To give every thread a different executable file","D":"To track its own active function calls and local execution data"}', 'D',
     'Threads have independent call sequences and local execution state, so they normally maintain separate stacks while sharing process memory such as the heap.', 'Threads'),
    ('A process arrives at time 2 and first receives CPU time at time 7. What is its response time?',
     '{"A":"5 time units","B":"7 time units","C":"9 time units","D":"2 time units"}', 'A',
     'Response time is the time of the first CPU service minus arrival time: 7 minus 2 equals 5.', 'CPU Scheduling'),
    ('A process has turnaround time 15 and uses 6 units of CPU time. If it never blocks for I/O, how long does it spend waiting in the ready queue?',
     '{"A":"21 time units","B":"9 time units","C":"6 time units","D":"15 time units"}', 'B',
     'With no I/O blocking, turnaround time consists of CPU time plus ready-queue waiting time. The waiting time is 15 minus 6 equals 9.', 'CPU Scheduling'),
    ('Under nonpreemptive first-come, first-served scheduling, one long CPU-bound job runs before several short jobs. What problem can result?',
     '{"A":"All short jobs must execute in parallel","B":"The long job is automatically interrupted after one instruction","C":"The short jobs wait behind the long job, producing a convoy effect","D":"Ready-queue waiting time becomes zero"}', 'C',
     'FCFS lets the running job continue until it blocks or finishes. Short jobs can experience long waits behind a long CPU burst, a convoy effect.', 'CPU Scheduling'),
    ('With round-robin scheduling and nonzero context-switch cost, what is a likely effect of making the time quantum extremely small?',
     '{"A":"Context switches disappear","B":"Every process finishes in one quantum","C":"The scheduler becomes nonpreemptive","D":"A larger fraction of CPU time is spent switching contexts"}', 'D',
     'Very small quanta cause frequent preemptions. With a nonzero switching cost, this increases scheduling overhead relative to useful execution.', 'CPU Scheduling'),
    ('A low-priority ready process keeps being passed over as higher-priority work arrives. Which technique can reduce this starvation?',
     '{"A":"Aging: gradually raising the priority of waiting processes","B":"Always lowering the priority of waiting processes","C":"Removing the process from the ready queue forever","D":"Disabling all priority changes while it waits"}', 'A',
     'Aging increases a waiting process priority over time, improving its chance of receiving CPU service despite a stream of higher-priority arrivals.', 'CPU Scheduling'),
    ('Under shortest-remaining-time-first scheduling, the running job has 8 units left when a new job needing 3 units arrives. Ignoring switching cost, what happens?',
     '{"A":"The running job must finish before any other job runs","B":"The new job preempts the running job because its remaining time is shorter","C":"The new job is discarded","D":"Both jobs run simultaneously on the same single core"}', 'B',
     'Shortest-remaining-time-first is preemptive. Assuming these are the only ready jobs and burst lengths are known, the job with 3 units runs before the job with 8 units remaining.', 'CPU Scheduling')
) AS seed(prompt, options, answer, explanation, concept_name)
JOIN concepts c ON c.name = seed.concept_name;
